  P0.3 -- document-boundary split (separator id 100257, 700 chunk-instances)
  A carry can only transfer information that EXISTS across the
  boundary.  Where the packer joined two unrelated documents
  there is nothing to carry: those instances sit in the
  denominator of every carry number the project has quoted and
  can never be in the numerator.  This sizes issue 7 and the mix.

  scheme 'local': across-document := a separator inside the 512 tokens before the boundary
    within-document   n=  406 (58.0%)  k64=+0.1108  k128=+0.1430  k256=+0.1795  k512=+0.2158
    across-document   n=  294 (42.0%)  k64=+0.1003  k128=+0.1216  k256=+0.1365  k512=+0.1288

  scheme 'prefix': across-document := a separator anywhere in chunks 1..g-1
    within-document   n=  202 (28.9%)  k64=+0.1198  k128=+0.1556  k256=+0.1956  k512=+0.2334
    across-document   n=  498 (71.1%)  k64=+0.1010  k128=+0.1252  k256=+0.1476  k512=+0.1573

  READ IT AS: an across-document ceiling near zero against a
  large within-document ceiling means the pack's real cross-chunk
  signal is only its within-document share -- the MIX RATIO, not
  the mechanism, is then what limits the carry.  Both ceilings
  large means the packer is not the problem and issue 7 shrinks.

  position bands inside the chunk (n=700 chunk-instances), delta vs k=0 in the SAME band:
  condition            0-25%      25-50%      50-75%     75-100%
  k64               +0.36736    +0.03458    +0.01596    +0.00769
  k128              +0.44130    +0.05517    +0.02580    +0.01369
  k256              +0.50631    +0.07983    +0.03864    +0.02095
  k512              +0.54826    +0.09656    +0.04677    +0.02547
  Compare the carry's PROFILE to the oracle's, not to flat.  A
  front-loaded oracle is the boundary effect; a carry that tracks
  it is stitching the seam, a carry that stays flat across bands
  is supplying something that is not the previous chunk's text.

